== P3: second-order self-interaction, exact ==
  d1(w) = theta^w(1 - w kappa) verified, w = 3..12: worst 1.4e-38
  CLOSED-FORM d2(w) (derived, second-order perturbation of the
  corpus fixed point): verified against numerics, w = 3..12:
  worst |d2_num - d2_formula| = 1.9e-26
  structure: d2(w)/theta^(2w) = -1/2 + b w + c w^2, with the
  w^2 coefficient c = eta (1-theta^2)^3 / (theta^2 Delta^2)
    c = 0.46254616774249620623
    numeric fit: a = -0.5 (exact -1/2: 8.5e-25),
                 c matches closed form at 2.1e-26
    b = -0.87755242177457519539 (closed form assembled inside
    d2_formula; b alone = kappa-weighted mix of the h2 pieces)

== P3b: does second order renormalize eps toward eps(1-eps)? ==
  eps (registered, first order) = 0.0317686364466
  two-order: -(d1+d2)/theta^3 = -0.133807508972
  exact: -defect(3)/theta^3 = -0.0525041043352
  TARGET eps(1-eps) = 0.0307593901849
  d2(3)/d1(3) = -5.2119374307  (the self-interaction
  flips the sign of the w=3 defect: binding is second order,
  exactly as P8 states; no two-order reading lands at eps(1-eps))
  VERDICT P3b: the single-relation second order does NOT produce
  the (1-eps) factor; if the dressed base is real, it must come
  from the LADDER bridge (rung composition), not from the defect
  expansion of one relation.  This is a constraint, not a defeat:
  it eliminates the most obvious derivation route.

